---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Building Your First AI Workflow with Notion — AI Agent Puzzle"
  description="Starting from Notion, run through the most complete and easiest-to-follow AI workflow: Search → Read → Create → Update. Not just plugging in an MCP, but actually seeing how an AI agent uses tools step by step to finish a task."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-notion-workflow">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">Letting AI Actually Get Things Done for You in Notion — For the First Time</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          You've just finished something important: installing Gemini CLI, opening it, and connecting your first off-the-shelf MCP. From here on, we're <strong>not just confirming it "connects" — we're going to have it actually complete one full round of work</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against Gemini CLI v0.55.1. npx always pulls the latest version, so the version number you see may be newer than the one here — small differences on screen are normal.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Before class:</strong> this whole page runs on Notion, so <strong>register an account and sign in beforehand</strong>. Signing up on the spot and waiting for a verification email will eat most of the session.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Why Notion -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Why Notion Is My Pick for the First Real Workflow</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If this is your first time letting AI actually help you get work done, I'd strongly suggest starting with Notion, rather than jumping into something overly complicated. The reason is simple: <strong>Notion's units of work are very intuitive</strong> — pages, databases, text content, field states. You can easily tell "what it found, what it read, what it created, what it changed."
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        It's very similar to the way we normally do knowledge work: <strong>first find the material, then understand it, then organize it into new content, and finally revise it once</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Page goal -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">What This Page Aims to Do</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This page isn't trying to teach you every Notion trick at once, and it isn't asking you to hand your entire workspace over to AI. This page does just one thing:
      </p>

      <div class="workflow-card">
        <p class="workflow-label">YOUR FIRST COMPLETE 4-STEP WORKFLOW</p>
        <p class="workflow-text">
          <span class="workflow-step">Search</span>
          <span class="workflow-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="workflow-step">Read</span>
          <span class="workflow-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="workflow-step">Create</span>
          <span class="workflow-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="workflow-step">Update</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If you can smoothly finish this one round, that's already plenty. Because it means you aren't just wiring up a tool — you're <strong>actually starting to understand how an AI agent uses tools step by step to complete a task</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Prerequisites -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Before You Start, Confirm These Three Things</h2>

      <ol class="prereq-list">
        <li class="prereq-item">
          <span class="prereq-num">1</span>
          <p class="prereq-text">You've already <strong>installed Gemini CLI</strong>, and it opens properly.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="prereq-item">
          <span class="prereq-num">2</span>
          <p class="prereq-text">You've already <strong>connected the Notion MCP</strong> and completed authorization.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="prereq-item">
          <span class="prereq-num">3</span>
          <p class="prereq-text">Your Notion <strong>has a few pages you can test against</strong>. They should be pages whose content you recognize and whose location you know, so you can judge whether AI got it right. If you don't have any yet, three ready-made ones are below.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">No test pages yet? Create these three first</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Add three pages in Notion, copying the titles and content exactly. With all three in place, everyone works from the same material, which is what makes comparing results meaningful.
      </p>

      <div class="sample-list">
        <article class="sample-card">
          <p class="sample-tag">Page 1</p>
          <h4 class="sample-title">Course Outline | AI Agent Practicum</h4>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>[Assignment] Final project: build your own MCP Server

[Requirements]
- Implement at least two tools
- Attach the contents of one settings.json
- Explain the least-privilege design reasoning
- 3 to 5 pages

[Deadlines]
- Proposal: 11/15
- Full report: 12/20</code></pre>
        </article>

        <article class="sample-card">
          <p class="sample-tag">Page 2</p>
          <h4 class="sample-title">Course Announcement | Week 3 Make-up Class</h4>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>Class on 10/8 is cancelled; the make-up session moves to the afternoon of 10/15.</code></pre>
        </article>

        <article class="sample-card">
          <p class="sample-tag">Page 3</p>
          <h4 class="sample-title">Reading Notes | The Three MCP Primitives</h4>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>Tools: the model decides when to call them. Resources: the application decides when to supply them. Prompts: the user chooses them deliberately.</code></pre>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>These three are designed this way on purpose.</strong> When you search for "course", the titles of the first two match directly; Page 3's content is about Tools, Resources, and Prompts and contains no mention of "course" at all, so it won't be found — which is exactly what STEP 1 below asks you to watch for. And Page 1 deliberately carries three kinds of information (assignment, requirements, deadlines) so STEP 4's "reorganize into three sections" has real material to work with.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          You may well see more than two results, and that's normal. Notion searches <strong>titles and content together</strong>, so other pages in your workspace that mention "course" will show up too; and if you created these three under some other page, that <strong>parent page</strong> usually gets pulled in as well. The point isn't whether the count is right — it's <strong>whether what should have been found was found, and whether anything clearly unrelated got mixed in</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Once the pages are created you can start right away. In most cases a new page is searchable immediately — Notion's indexing takes seconds at worst. If your first search turns up nothing, just wait a moment and try again; that isn't your mistake.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 1: Search -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 1</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Learn to Search Pages First</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        For a first workflow, the best starting point is <strong>search</strong>. Search is a low-risk action whose results are easy to evaluate — you can tell at a glance whether it found the right thing.
      </p>

      <div class="tool-box">
        <p class="tool-box__label">Notion MCP supported tool</p>
        <p class="tool-box__name"><code>notion-search</code></p>
        <p class="tool-box__desc">
          Searches your Notion workspace; if you have Notion AI, it can also search connected sources like Slack, Google Drive, and Jira.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>This course uses a free account, so search covers the Notion workspace only.</strong> You'll get fewer results than you might expect — that's an account-tier difference, not something you did wrong.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can start by saying something like this to Gemini CLI:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please search Notion for pages related to "courses" first — don't modify anything for now.
      </blockquote>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">Or:</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Find pages whose title or content mentions "final report", and list the 3 most relevant ones.
      </blockquote>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The point of this step isn't to have it do many things at once. It's to <strong>first see whether it can find the right pages</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">The first time it calls a tool, it asks you first</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        After you send your instruction, nothing starts happening right away — a yellow confirmation box appears first:
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/工具執行確認框.png"
          alt="Gemini CLI asking whether to allow a Notion MCP tool to run"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The authorization prompt on the first call to a given tool</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>This isn't an error, and it isn't stuck.</strong> This is Gemini CLI telling you which tool the AI intends to use and what arguments it's carrying, and asking you to confirm. That line in the box — <code is:raw>notion-search {"query":"課程","query_type":"internal"}</code> — is the full payload it's about to send. It's worth a glance: this is your only chance to stop it.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The four options differ like this:
      </p>

      <div class="csv-table-wrapper">
        <table class="csv-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Option</th>
              <th>What it actually does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1. Allow once</strong></td>
              <td>Allows <strong>this one call</strong> only. The next call to the same tool asks again.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>2. Allow tool for this session</strong></td>
              <td>This tool stops asking for the rest of the session, but a different tool still asks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>3. Allow all server tools for this session</strong></td>
              <td>Allows <strong>all 28 tools</strong> under this server; nothing asks again for the rest of the session.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>4. No, suggest changes (esc)</strong></td>
              <td>Doesn't run it, and hands control back to you so you can revise the instruction and try again.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        "Session" here means <strong>this stretch of time with Gemini CLI open</strong>. Close and reopen it, or run <code>/clear</code>, and permissions start over.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Three things worth knowing:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Option 3 costs more than it looks.</strong> You only wanted it to search, but that one keypress also allows deleting, moving, and creating databases — tools you have no use for today. Convenient, but the scope goes to maximum in one step.</li>
        <li><strong>Option 4 is the real brake.</strong> Many people assume the only choice is between "agree" and "quit". When the arguments look wrong — a mistyped keyword, the wrong page about to be edited — pick 4. That option exists precisely for you to use.</li>
        <li><strong>The four steps on this page use four different tools</strong>, so even if you pick 2 every time, the next step will still ask again when it reaches a new tool. Each prompt is a checkpoint on "what is the AI about to do" — don't click through too fast.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        I'd suggest picking <strong>1</strong> the first time, so you get a feel for what "asks every time" is like, and <strong>2</strong> for the steps after that.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">What to look for when searching</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        On your first search, don't just check "did it give me a list of answers." What you should look at:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Are the pages it found <strong>ones you actually recognize</strong></li>
        <li>Are the keywords it picked up reasonable</li>
        <li>Did it pull in irrelevant things too (a parent page tagging along is normal — what to watch for is a result whose content is clearly unrelated)</li>
        <li>Did it respect your instruction to "<strong>search only, don't modify</strong>"</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If this step is unstable, reading, creating, and updating pages afterward all tend to go off track. So for a first workflow, <strong>search is actually the most important step</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2: Read -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 2</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">From Finding a Page to Actually Reading It</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Searching only finds the entry point — it doesn't really understand the content. So Step 2 is <strong>opening up one page and reading it</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="tool-box">
        <p class="tool-box__label">Notion MCP supported tool</p>
        <p class="tool-box__name"><code>notion-fetch</code></p>
        <p class="tool-box__desc">
          Fetches page content by URL or ID, and can also retrieve details of a database or data source.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can follow up with something like:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please open the most relevant page from earlier and summarize its key points for me — don't create a new page yet.
      </blockquote>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">Or something more specific:</p>
      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read the page I just mentioned and tell me what points it contains that are related to the final report requirements.
      </blockquote>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Two blocks show up on screen — don't let the first one scare you</h3>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/notion-fetch原始回傳.png"
          alt="The raw response from notion-fetch, with the model's organized summary below it"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The top half is the tool's raw response; the bottom half is what the model organized for you</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That big messy-looking block inside the box — <code>\n</code>, <code>&lt;page&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;properties&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;content&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;empty-block/&gt;</code> — is <strong>the raw data the Notion tool handed back untouched</strong>. That format is written for the model to read, not for you. You don't need to make sense of it.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The part outside the box, the one starting with a purple star, is <strong>the model's answer after it finished reading</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This distinction is useful. On the previous page we separated "PowerShell" from "inside the CLI"; here we're separating "the tool's output" from "the model's answer". When a result isn't what you expected, this tells you which half went wrong — the tool finding nothing at all, and the tool finding it but the model summarizing it badly, are two completely different problems, and they call for different fixes.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">You don't need to go hunting for the page ID</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>notion-fetch</code> needs to know which page to fetch, but you don't have to copy a long ID out of Notion yourself. The results from the previous step already carry each page's URL, and the model passes that URL straight through as the argument — in the confirmation box you'll see something like <code is:raw>{"id":"https://app.notion.com/p/..."}</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This is why the order "search first, then read" is meaningful: <strong>the output of step one is exactly the input of step two</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        One thing worth mentioning in passing: the response contains an <code>ancestor-path</code> section, which hands over the <strong>title and URL of this page's parent</strong> as well. You only asked it to read one page, but the page's location went out with it. This isn't a bug — it's how the tool is designed, and the Least Privilege &amp; Security Boundaries page later on comes back to exactly this: <strong>what goes out is usually more than you assumed</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The point of this step is to get AI <strong>not just to "find the page," but to actually do something based on the page's content</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">When reading, don't just ask "what does it say"</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Better questions look like:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>What points here could I later turn into a report?</li>
        <li>What are the explicit requirements, steps, or deadlines on this page?</li>
        <li>Which parts of this page are good candidates for a summary?</li>
        <li>What content on this page is suitable for turning into a new page?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        With questions like these, once AI finishes reading, it flows more naturally into the next step: <strong>creating a new page</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Step 3: Create -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 3</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Based on What It Read, Create a New Page</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This step is <strong>the first real "write"</strong>. Instead of just looking at data, you're letting AI create a new page in Notion for you.
      </p>

      <div class="tool-box">
        <p class="tool-box__label">Notion MCP supported tool</p>
        <p class="tool-box__name"><code>notion-create-pages</code></p>
        <p class="tool-box__desc">
          Creates one or more pages, and can also specify properties, content, icons, and even supports templates. If no parent is specified, the system creates a private page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        For your first page creation, I suggest the simplest possible version:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Based on the content of that page, create a new Notion page titled "Final Report Key Points" — start with a bullet-list draft.
      </blockquote>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          This step gives you your first clear sense that: <strong>AI isn't just looking at data — it's actually producing new content for you.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Once it's created, go find where it landed</h3>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/新頁面在私人區.png"
          alt="The Notion sidebar, with the newly created page at the top of the Private section"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The new page doesn't land under the page you started from. What matters here is the location in the sidebar on the left — the content on the right is already the STEP 4 version</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Many people instinctively assume the new page will sit next to "Course Outline", and then can't find it where they expected. Because the instruction didn't specify a parent, Notion creates it as a <strong>top-level private page</strong> — look under the "Private" section of the sidebar. The page title also carries a "Private" lock marker beside it.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If you want it somewhere specific, say so in the instruction — for example, "create it under the Course Outline page."
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">For the first time, smaller is better</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        At this step, many people can't resist asking:
      </p>
      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li>Just write me the full report</li>
        <li>Organize all my notes for the entire course</li>
        <li>Build me a whole final-project outline</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Those aren't impossible — they're just <strong>not appropriate for the first time</strong>. For a first page creation, the best target is:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>A smaller page</li>
        <li>Shorter content</li>
        <li>A clearer, more focused topic</li>
        <li>Something you can clearly see how it was organized</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Because on the first round, what matters most isn't volume — it's that you can really see: <strong>how it moved from "search" and "read" to "create"</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Step 4: Update -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 4</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Update the Page You Just Created</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Step 4 isn't about redoing a new page — it's about <strong>making small edits to the page you just created</strong>. This step matters because it lets you feel that "update" and "create" are actually two different kinds of work.
      </p>

      <div class="tool-box">
        <p class="tool-box__label">Notion MCP supported tool</p>
        <p class="tool-box__name"><code>notion-update-page</code></p>
        <p class="tool-box__desc">
          Updates page properties or content based on what was found or created in the previous step.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can do something like:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please do three things to the "Final Report Key Points" page you just created: add a summary of 50 characters or less at the top, reorganize the content into three sections — "Topic", "Requirements", and "Deadline" — and don't delete the original bullet list.
      </blockquote>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This kind of update is great for beginners, because you can clearly see:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Which parts are the <strong>original content</strong></li>
        <li>Which parts were <strong>changed afterward</strong></li>
        <li>Whether AI only touched the scope you specified</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Why "don't delete the original content" has to be written down</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In the confirmation box you'll see that <code>notion-update-page</code> takes a <code>new_str</code> argument — <strong>what it sends is the complete rewritten content of the whole page, not just the few lines you wanted changed</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That means something: the original bullets are still there because <strong>the model retyped the old content and wrote it all back</strong>, not because the tool protected them for you. If the model drops a section, that section is simply gone — and there's no warning of any kind.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This is why the "read" in STEP 2 can't be skipped. It has to read the page in full before it has any way to write it back in full.
      </p>
      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>Read first, then write</strong> — this is what that phrase is actually about.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">When updating, more specific = safer</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        For a first update, the thing to fear isn't that it won't make changes — it's that <strong>it changes too much</strong>. So when writing your instruction, spell out three things:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Which page to modify</li>
        <li>Which paragraph to modify</li>
        <li>Which places not to touch</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Section: When it goes wrong -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">If It Gets It Wrong, That Doesn't Mean the Approach Is Broken</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        For a first workflow, <strong>mistakes are normal</strong>. Common problems look like:
      </p>
      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li>The pages it found weren't accurate enough</li>
        <li>The points it pulled out were too scattered</li>
        <li>The new page it created was too empty</li>
        <li>The update changed too much</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        At that moment, don't immediately conclude "Notion isn't right" or "AI can't handle this." A better move is to <strong>look back and see which step went off</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="reflect-list">
        <li class="reflect-item">
          <span class="reflect-num">1</span>
          <span class="reflect-text">Were the search terms too vague?</span>
        </li>
        <li class="reflect-item">
          <span class="reflect-num">2</span>
          <span class="reflect-text">Was the read instruction too broad?</span>
        </li>
        <li class="reflect-item">
          <span class="reflect-num">3</span>
          <span class="reflect-text">Did you specify the format clearly when creating?</span>
        </li>
        <li class="reflect-item">
          <span class="reflect-num">4</span>
          <span class="reflect-text">Did you set clear boundaries when updating?</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          That way, you'll pinpoint the problem faster, and <strong>it'll be easier to make the next round more solid.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ai-agent-notion-workflow {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Header */
  .detail-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .detail-title {
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-heading);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .detail-subtitle {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .detail-subtitle strong {
    color: var(--color-heading);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* Section headings */
  .detail-heading {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
